# What never reaches a build context.
#
# Every image in docker/ builds with the repository root as its context, and
# a context is packed and handed to the daemon before the first instruction
# runs. Measured over this repository's working tree, that transfer is
# 1.86GB without this file and 844kB with it: 240MB of packet captures under
# CryptomonData/ plus the 240MB of zips they came out of, 13MB more under
# sandbox/, a 690MB monitor dump beside those, and 570MB of git history. No
# image needs any of it.
#
# BuildKit is cleverer than that figure suggests -- when a Dockerfile's COPY
# lines name specific paths, as all three of these do, it transfers only
# those paths. The 1.86GB is what the classic builder pays, what any
# `COPY . .` pays, and what the first careless line added to any of these
# files would pay.
#
# It is also a disclosure control, and that is the half worth keeping when
# somebody later decides the size no longer matters. `COPY . .` copies
# whatever happens to be lying in the working tree, and in this repository
# that is other people's packet captures and MongoDB dumps. A capture's SNI
# field is browsing history -- fapi/app/uploads.py says so at length, and
# fapi/app/retention.py expires reports for exactly that reason. A layer
# expires never, and is pushed to whichever registry somebody points at it.
# So the captures are excluded here rather than left to each Dockerfile's
# COPY lines being careful.
#
# Docker's patterns are matched against the whole relative path and `*` does
# not cross a `/`, so a rule meant to apply at any depth is written `**/`.
# `CryptomonData` on its own excludes the directory and everything under it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The corpora. This is the 254MB of captures and the 690MB dump.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CryptomonData
sandbox

# Captures anywhere, including tests/fixtures. Nothing in any image reads a
# capture from inside itself: the offline analyser is given one on a bind
# mount, which is also the only way it can be given one that is not in this
# repository. Keeping them out means a stray capture dropped in the working
# tree cannot be baked into an image by accident.
**/*.pcap
**/*.pcapng
**/*.pcap.gz
**/*.pcapng.gz
**/*.zip

# TinyDB's on-disk database, written by a monitor run without MongoDB. Same
# contents as the collection: handshakes, addresses and server names.
cryptomon.json
**/cryptomon-*.json
**/monitor-*.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Version control, local state and editor droppings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.git
.gitignore
.github
.claude
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*$py.class
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov
**/.DS_Store

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anything that could carry a credential
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .env is how compose is configured (docker/README.md), and compose reads it
# from the host -- it has no business inside an image. docker/env.example is
# deliberately named without the dot so that it is not caught here and not
# mistaken for a live one. The service unit written by create-service.sh
# carries DB_URL with the password in it.
.env
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.pem
**/*.key
cryptomon.service
config-secrets.sh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not needed by any image
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Dockerfiles copy the packages and entry points they need by name, so
# these are belt and braces -- but a context small enough to read is a
# context somebody will check before adding a COPY line.
venv
.venv
img
docs
DEVELOPMENT.md
PULL_REQUESTS.md
